<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arcos de la Frontera</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arcos de la Frontera is a hilltop town in the province of Cádiz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, set on a sandstone ridge above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalete">Guadalete</a> river. In the 11th century it was the seat of a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifa</a> under the Banu Khizrun until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbadid-dynasty">Abbadids</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> annexed it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> conquered it in the mid 13th century, and it became a frontier town facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arcos-de-la-frontera.jpg" alt="Arcos de la Frontera" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luis Rogelio HM · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arcos_de_la_Frontera_-_001_(30591512202).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
